Thought: To identify the organic compound and provide its SMILES code, I should start by gathering some initial data about the compound. The mass spectrometry spectra will give me the molecular weight and possible fragments, which is a good starting point for identifying the compound.
Action: mass_spectrometry_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The mass spectrometry data indicates a molecular ion peak at m/z 193.11, which suggests the molecular weight of the compound is approximately 193.11 g/mol. To further narrow down the possibilities, I should obtain the molecular formula using this information. The carbon NMR spectra can provide insights into the carbon framework of the compound.
Action: carbon_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Since the carbon NMR prediction failed, I should try another approach to deduce the molecular formula. The proton NMR spectra might provide useful information about the hydrogen environment, which can help in deducing the structure of the compound.
Action: proton_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Both the carbon and proton NMR predictions have failed, which limits the direct structural information I can obtain from these spectra. Given the molecular weight from the mass spectrometry data, I should attempt to deduce the molecular formula and possible isomers. I will use the molecular weight to estimate the molecular formula and then find possible isomers.
Action: obtain_isomers_from_molecular_formula
Action Input: {"molecular_formula": "C12H16O2"}

Thought: I have obtained a list of possible isomers for the molecular formula C12H16O2. To determine which isomer matches the sample, I should validate each SMILES string and check if any additional data can help confirm the correct structure. I will start by validating the SMILES strings.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC[C@H](CC1=CC=C(C=C1)C)C(=O)O"}

Thought: The first SMILES string is valid. I will proceed to validate the next SMILES string to ensure all potential isomers are considered.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC(CO)C1=CC=C(C=C1)CCC=O"}

Thought: The second SMILES string is also valid. I will continue validating the remaining SMILES strings to ensure all potential isomers are considered.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C1C[C@H]([C@H](C1)O)[C@@H](C2=CC=CC=C2)O"}

Thought: The third SMILES string is valid. I will proceed to validate the fourth SMILES string.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC1=CC(=C(C=C1)OC2CC2)[C@@H](C)O"}

Thought: The fourth SMILES string is valid. I will now validate the fifth and final SMILES string.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C[C@@H]1[C@@H](OC(O1)CC2=CC=CC=C2)C"}
